g those  game-changing connections  keep believing that this is a crime that  can be ended once and for all  because I know with your commitment it  can  thank you  Sky News is Royal correspondent Rhianna  Mills was at the summit  well it shouldn't be underestimated the  fact that the new Prince of Wales and  now Earth the throne has decided that  this is the issue that he wants to talk  about in his first speech and it is a  passionate speech where in no uncertain  terms he is talking very clearly about  the dangers of not tackling the illegal  Wildlife trade he talks about how too  many criminals believe they can act with  impunity too many lives are being  destroyed and too many species are on  the brink of Extinction and I think he's  very clearly making that point  emphasizing that yes of course the  destruction of wildlife is really  important but it's also the knock-on  impact the fact that the trade is often  funding serious and organized crime  terrorism around the world and that's  why it's important for both the public  the private sector businesses and  governments all to get together when it  comes to tackling this issue now United  for wildlife that's being talked about  today actually Prince William's been  involved in for six years now and it has  had its successes which told that it's  contributed to 250 arrests at 450 cases  of illegal Wildlife trading and also  trained a hundred thousand people to try  and Tackle this issue but at the center  of it I think again you're seeing how a  member of the royal family is really  important when it comes to being a  convening power getting people around  the table and of course that's something  we've already talked about this week in  the context of we now know that the king  is now no longer going to go to cop 27  of course lots of debate about why  exactly he's decided not to go whether  it was the government who suggested he  shouldn't or whether it was his own team  who thought that it wasn't the right 